[h1]Flavorium: Diseases+[/h1]

[i]Compatible with EU5 1.3.* | Standalone, no other Flavorium mod required[/i]

[hr][/hr]

A permanent rebalance of smallpox and Old World epidemic resistance, for players who want outbreaks to stay a significant threat without the early-game population collapse. Smallpox still spreads and still becomes endemic; it just no longer removes a quarter of a region's people the first time it arrives.

This started as a hotfix during the 1.3 beta. The values held up in long games, so it now ships as a standing alternative rather than a temporary patch.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Starting disease resistance[/h2]

Old World locations begin with higher acquired resistance, on the reasoning that those populations had lived alongside these diseases for centuries. In observation, resistance climbs to a similar equilibrium within a few decades of play regardless. This starts it near that observed floor instead of modeling first contact for populations that had none.

[list]
[*] [b]Smallpox:[/b] 0.50 becomes 0.75
[*] [b]Measles, influenza and typhus:[/b] 0.30 becomes 0.65
[/list]

Applies to Europe, Asia and Africa. New World populations are untouched, so colonial contact stays as lethal as vanilla intends.

[h2]Smallpox behavior[/h2]

[list]
[*] [b]Mortality rate:[/b] 0.05 to 0.30 becomes 0.001 to 0.25, moving the average from roughly 17.5% to roughly 12.5%. That sits nearer published endemic case-fatality estimates than vanilla's figure, which reflects a virgin-soil epidemic.
[*] [b]Outbreaks begin in settled places:[/b] vanilla can spawn in any location with a population above 1. Spawning now requires an urbanized location, a rural one above 5,000, or a coastal one above 2,000.
[*] [b]Outbreaks matter when they happen:[/b] spawn strength 0.50 becomes 0.85, above the threshold for significant presence, so an outbreak reliably reaches adjacent locations instead of quietly dying out.
[*] [b]Spawn frequency:[/b] 0.08 becomes 0.10 per month. Slightly more outbreaks, each one consequential.
[*] [b]Stagnation:[/b] raised the maximum stagnation chance where the disease is already present, and removed a duplicated multiplier in that condition.
[/list]

The net effect is fewer trivial outbreaks in empty countryside and fewer catastrophic ones in your capital, with smallpox settling into a persistent endemic background instead of a single demographic shock.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]What it does not change[/h2]

[list]
[*] Bubonic plague, great pestilence and malaria. Vanilla already accounts for prior exposure in their values.
[*] New World starting resistance.
[*] Anything from the other Flavorium mods. This runs on its own or alongside them.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[i]Feedback on whether the numbers feel right is welcome on the discussion page.[/i]
